   "creator_full": null,
   "caption": "The CNBC fight that cracked Wall Street on live TV.\nOn March 31, 2014, Brad Katsuyama went on CNBC and publicly accused the market of being rigged \u2014 sitting feet away from William O\u2019Brien, a senior executive at BATS Global Markets.\nThe backdrop wasn\u2019t drama \u2014 it was data.\nAt the time, high-frequency firms were responsible for 50\u201360% of all U.S. equity trading volume, using speed advantages measured in microseconds to detect and trade ahead of slower orders.\nKatsuyama claimed exchanges were selling:\n\u2022 Proprietary data feeds\n\u2022 Co-location (servers inside exchange data centers)\n\u2022 Complex order types\n...creating a two-tier market:\none for institutions and one for machines.\nO\u2019Brien denied it on air.\nDays later, BATS issued a public clarification admitting some matching logic relied on slower public SIP data feeds, contradicting what was said live.\nThis wasn\u2019t a debate.\nIt was a system getting exposed in real time.\n\ud83c\udfa5 Source: \u201cFight Stops NYSE Traders | Archives | CNBC\u201d (CNBC YouTube)\n\n\ud83d\udc49Follow @itsfinancenote for more !",
